{
  "version": "schemaxis.readiness/1",
  "routes": {
    "schemaxis.r/1": {
      "guide": "docs/r.md",
      "context": {
        "purpose": "Fit a small numeric calibration or explanatory model, optionally quantify classical coefficient uncertainty, and decide whether actual finite training/held-out fit/prediction results and coefficient intervals meet named requirements.",
        "assumptions": "Unweighted additive numeric ordinary least squares. Rows use declared predictor order followed by observed response, with plain unit labels. Fixed native symbols and vanilla Rscript; fresh RDS replay, independent QR verification. Optional inference assumes a correct additive mean, independent constant-variance normal errors and fixed predictors. Optional prediction intervals for declared new predictor-only rows assume fixed error-free predictors, a correct additive mean and independent constant-variance normal errors.",
        "constraints": "One to eight predictors, four to 512 training rows and optional one to 512 separate held-out rows. Training rows exceed coefficient count; full rank and actual scaled rectangular infinity-norm condition at most1e6. Inference level 0.5 to 0.999 and residual scatter at least 1e6 times the comparison roundoff allowance. No categorical/missing values, arbitrary formulas/scripts/models, weights, interactions, prediction intervals, multiplicity adjustment or generalisation guarantee.",
        "inspection": "Review coefficients with original predictor/response units in regression-summary.json, actual training/held-out residuals in CSV and each signed requirement margin/worst sampled condition. With inference, review standard errors, t, p, intervals, residual degrees of freedom, R-squared basis and stated assumptions in the summary and inference.csv. Native comparison uses relative1e-10 plus128*ULP(max training response magnitude)*max(1,scaled condition); inference uses 1e-4 relative with propagated p-value intervals and exact level/df readback. With prediction, review prediction.csv and the summary prediction block: input row identity and units, fit, requested interval, leverage, extrapolation flags, residual degrees of freedom and sigma; native confidence and individual intervals are both compared with independent QR leverage at 1e-5 relative.",
        "recovery": "MISSING_VALUE (null/NaN/NA cell) needs the incomplete row removed or the measurement supplied; UNSUPPORTED_VALUE_TYPE (text/boolean cell) needs a plain numeric measurement. Neither is coerced or imputed. RANK_DEFICIENT_DATA/ILL_CONDITIONED_DATA/INSUFFICIENT_DATA need independent predictors and a supported design. NO_RESIDUAL_VARIANCE means an exact or roundoff-level fit: remove inference or use measured data with scatter. UNSUPPORTED_INFERENCE needs a rescaled response. INVALID_REQUIREMENT needs valid scopes/indices/terms/bounds and inference for coefficient intervals. FIT_REQUIREMENT_NOT_MET retains valid fit evidence and needs review of data/model or justified requirements then a fresh attempt. Configure qualified Rscript for INTERPRETER_NOT_FOUND; retain logs for native faults. INVALID_TABLE for a new-data row of the wrong width needs predictor values only in declared order. NO_RESIDUAL_VARIANCE also refuses prediction intervals for an exact or roundoff-level fit. UNSUPPORTED_PREDICTION at $.prediction.newdata[i] means that row's interval types cannot be verified apart: its leverage exceeds 1e4 or its confidence and individual half widths differ by less than four times the comparison allowance plus relative component. It is a verification-resolution limit, not model validity: supply residual scatter that is larger relative to the response magnitude and design scale (smaller offset, centred predictors) or move the row toward the training design. INVALID_PREDICTION means the projected prediction or interval exceeds the numeric table budget."
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "baseline",
          "reference": "examples/schemaxis/r-regression.json"
        },
